--- a/docs/architecture.md
+++ b/docs/architecture.md
@@ -200,3 +200,5 @@ The screen container uses the original world origin at stage275,225, outside cam
 `core/part-unlocks.ts` models the source per-world unlock reset and cumulative upgrade-menu grants. `World.getOffers` updates the session list before candidate filtering; damage/downgrade leaves it intact. `GameShell` passes the snapshot into in-game creator props, and `TankCreator` forwards it unchanged to the viewer. Palette locks, build validation, loading eligibility and upgrade offers share membership checks while retaining separate point/class rules. Unlocks are not added to local profile storage: the source resets them on a new world. The isolated `unlock-retention` fixture uses real world damage and production creator/viewer components to verify that boundary.

 `core/profile-class.ts` separately models `PlayerProfile.maxClass`. `GameShell` owns one app-session instance and shares it across worlds. Each actual upgrade overwrites it with the new level plus one, capped at6; downgrades and new-world construction leave it intact. It is neither a peak-ever maximum nor a field inferred from saved progression. Creator rings and actual avatar load eligibility still use the current level plus one. The creator forwards the stored value only for the viewer's Current Points class readout and source rejection labels, whose class comparison differs from eligibility. The local profile schema remains unchanged because the source serialization omits this value. The fixture tests real upgrades/downgrades and the distinct world/profile lifetimes in both asset modes.
+
+`core/viewer-storage.ts` defines the viewer's source save identity: exact author/name plus each ordered part's name/key, type, twin, position, rotation, scale and radius. Local queue deduplication, saves and targeted deletion share that identity; IDs and tank statistics do not replace distinct designs. `ViewerPane.local` captures local-profile versus imported-code provenance and survives card/detail reparenting and fades. Saving a code preview does not turn it into a locally pulled preview. Save completion is keyed by pane and layout, using small frame3, detailed frame3 for a new save and frame4 for an existing match; copy completion also belongs to its individual control. `ViewerButton` can display those authored stopped states without substituting a generic disabled frame. `GameShell` and the separate offline library share append/remove adapters, and library row/confirmation identities distinguish designs with the same imported ID. Local JSON still physically removes deleted entries, rather than modeling the original profile's hidden-entry flag.
diff --git a/docs/fidelity.md b/docs/fidelity.md
index e60c544..cb56b3a 100644
--- a/docs/fidelity.md
+++ b/docs/fidelity.md
@@ -693,3 +693,12 @@ Keep this ledger current. Move a remaining item into verified coverage only afte
 - `core/profile-class.ts` holds this app-session field outside persisted local JSON. `GameShell` shares one instance across worlds: `BTAGameWorld.Setup` resets unlocks through `_‑cq` without resetting maxClass. A later actual upgrade overwrites the stored class. A fresh profile starts at1 through the source DataObject constructor, and `_‑Wu` does not serialize this field. Saved progression is therefore not used to infer it. The default standalone viewer/fixture adapter remains available to callers that omit the field.
 - Verification:529 unit/parser/rule tests with6222 assertions, TypeScript, formatting and a production build passed. All eight affected browser flows passed with mock and supplied-SWF assets. The new pair checks the level1 creator cap2 alongside viewer Class4, GP-only and empty-suffix rejection copy, re-earned level2/Class3, and a subsequent fresh world's first upgrade/Class2. Existing pairs cover unlock retention/reset, candidate-class eligibility and in-game editing. Four inspected captures are retained at `/tmp/bt3-{mock,native}-profile-class-{downgrade,reupgrade}.png`. React review covered app-session ownership, lazy initialization, creator/viewer prop transfer and paused-screen reads without per-frame React state. No generated metadata, artwork or persisted schema changed.
 - Cheat flags, complete profile/navigation entry points, exact global constructor/event timing, device-font metrics and synchronized original runtime recordings remain unfinished. These recovered contracts do not establish complete profile or game parity.
+
+## Viewer local-save identity and completion artwork
+
+- `TankViewerScreen.SaveTank` switches the compact Save control to frame3 (`Saved`) and removes its mouse input before checking for a duplicate. `SaveLargeTank` removes its listeners and uses frame3 (`Saved`) for a new entry, or frame4 (`Already Owned`) for a matching entry. `ViewerButton` now renders the corresponding authored widget frames in both asset modes. Small and detailed save completion belong to their separate control instances; page/setup reconstruction resets them through the source button-registration path.
+- `_‑6G` compares exact author/name and the ordered parts' name, type, twin, x, y, rotation, scale and radius. It does not compare tank ID, class, life, reward, speed or difficulty. `core/viewer-storage.ts` shares that comparison with the normal local save adapter and visible queue deduplication. An alias ID does not create another copy; distinct names or part data can coexist under the same imported ID. Save checks remain separated by avatar/enemy profile category.
+- `TankLoadingBox.Setup` hides Save for locally pulled `tankType1` objects, while a code import keeps its source type even after it is saved. Source type4 code previews also hide Delete. Each `ViewerPane` now retains its local/code origin through detail transfers, paging, sorting, code cancellation and fading shells. Saving no longer removes the imported preview's Save button because profile membership changed. Copy completion likewise uses control-instance identity so shared IDs do not couple unrelated controls.
+- Viewer deletion follows `_‑2a`'s first matching design comparison instead of removing every design with the same ID. The offline JSON library uses the same append/removal policy and distinct row/confirmation identities; imports and deletion preserve other designs that happen to share an ID. This adapter still physically removes the local record, rather than reproducing SharedObject hidden-entry flags and their later re-import behavior. That profile-lifecycle difference remains explicit; no persisted schema, original media or generated metadata changed.
+- Verification:534 unit/parser/rule tests with6245 assertions, TypeScript, formatting and a production build passed. All eight affected browser flows passed in mock and supplied-SWF modes. The new pair imports a built-in code and checks compact frame3/detailed frame4 without appending a copy, saves a new code while keeping its control visible, suppresses a different-ID/statistics alias, saves a distinct same-ID design through detailed frame3, verifies both local rows, deletes only the selected match, reloads persisted data and checks the offline library's same-ID import/deletion path. Existing pairs cover creator routing, local delete, code cancellation, toolbar fades and retained preview lifetimes. Four inspected captures are retained at `/tmp/bt3-{mock,native}-viewer-save-{duplicate,new}.png`. React review covered immutable pane provenance, per-control completion state, source-frame rendering, stable design row keys and whole-design callbacks rather than ambiguous ID callbacks.
+- Original hidden-profile-entry lifecycle, complete profile/navigation entry points, selection/notification edge cases, global constructor/event phase, device-font metrics and synchronized original-runtime recordings remain unfinished. These source-backed save contracts and offline adapters do not establish complete profile or game parity.
